body:
It's worth  comparing @mstensho's first testcase there to the same one but with an explicit  100px height on that  child div.  Here's a testcase that shows them side by side, along with a `<div>` to demonstrate "normal" sizing:

    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <style>
      fieldset, #control {
        border: 10px solid;
        padding:0;
        height:100px;
        width: 20%;
        display: inline-block;
      }
    
      legend {
        width:50px; height:50px;
      }
    
      div { background: yellow; }
    </style>
    <fieldset>
      <legend></legend>
      <div style="height:100%"></div>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset>
      <legend></legend>
      <div style="height:100px;"></div>
    </fieldset>
    <div id="control"></div>

In Safari, Edge, Firefox, and Chrome the bottom of the bottom fieldset border lines up with  the bottom of the bottom "control" div border.  This means that if the fieldset top border is being expanded, then something else must be contracting, and the something is the used content-box height (because paddings can't be negative, right?).

Now the behavior of the  `height: 100%` does vary across browsers.  Edge and Chrome treat that as "100% of the specified height" while Safari and Firefox treat it as "100% of the used height".  I seem to recall @MatsPalmgren having some changes in mind for the Firefox behavior in this regard?  Anyway, the 100% becomes "60px" in Safari and Firefox: The legend is 50px and the border is 10px, so the legend sticks out by 40px (20 top and 20 bottom) and the content area gets shrunk by that amount.

If I change the box-sizing on the fieldset to border-box, then all the browsers make the fieldset slightly smaller.  Now in Firefox and Safari 100% on the child becomes 40px (80px minus the 40px described above).  In Chrome and Edge 100% becomes 80px.  So that's all consistent with the content-box case.

